    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters today\u2019s slate leaning heavily on **James Wood** (76.28) and **Josh Naylor** (65.16) as our offensive anchors. **James Wood** is our premier play, benefiting from a high-upside matchup against Giolito (5.37 xERA) and the lead-off spot, providing an elite environment for both counting stats and efficiency. Similarly, **Josh Naylor** projects as our strongest internal asset against Gallen; despite a park penalty, his dynamic platoon advantage and current form make him an essential start. **Austin Riley** (64.99) also earns a high grade, as the matchup against Paddack offers a rare SP skill advantage that boosts his base projection into the top tier of our lineup.\n\nThe most difficult roster decision today involved **Vinnie Pasquantino**, who missed the cut with a 43.95 score. While his base projection remains competitive, the severe platoon handicap against Gore pushed him below our efficiency floor relative to the depth of our middle infield and utility slots. Elsewhere, we are capitalizing on aggressive baserunning opportunities: **Corbin Carroll** (62.47) and **Seiya Suzuki** (58.05) are both start-worthy, specifically because their matchups against Kirby and Pallante offer slow delivery windows that should catalyze our pursuit of the league\u2019s scarcest category, stolen bases. \n\nRisk management remains a priority given the volatility of the pitching matchups. While **Corbin Carroll** and **Gabriel Moreno** face the high-tier arm of Kirby (3.46 xERA), their dynamic platoon benefits keep them in the lineup, though owners should monitor for any late-inning defensive substitutions if the game turns into a blowout. **Jose Caballero** remains our most conditional start; while he provides stolen base utility, his score of 57.17 is dampened by a poor batting order spot and an elite defensive catcher, meaning his value is almost entirely contingent on his ability to reach base and exploit that slow delivery on the mound.",
